WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.529
A flashback to Pennywise's origins, a floating
dream sequence, and graphic scenes from the

00:00:05.529 --> 00:00:09.550
original script are just some of the things
that were cut from the final version of IT:

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:10.550
Chapter One.

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:14.380
Yippee-ki-yay movie lovers, it's Jan here
and today I'm delving into some of the horrifying

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:18.950
scenes, script changes, and rejected concepts
we never got to see in the new IT movie.

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:22.390
And I'll also be explaining how we can expect
to see a number of these deleted storylines

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:24.360
appear in the movie's sequel.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.500
My Pennywise Funko Pop giveaway is also running
on this video.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:31.790
So, for a chance to win, make sure you subscribe
to my channel and leave a comment below with

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:34.270
your thoughts about the movie or any of the
cut scenes.

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:38.000
Spoiler warning: I'm going to discuss scenes
from the movie and speculate on the sequel,

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.070
so take care if you haven't seen IT yet.

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:45.340
Given Henry Bowers' repeated, unhinged acts
of cruelty in the film, it's safe to say most

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:48.670
of the audience will have been glad to see
him get his/some sort of comeuppance.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:52.710
However, there were a couple of deleted scenes
that director Andy Muschietti revealed in

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:56.610
an interview with Vulture that he says would
have brought "some humanity to Henry."

00:00:56.610 --> 00:01:00.960
The director explained that the scenes depicted
"the conflict [Henry] has at home with his

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:06.130
father, how and why Henry is a monster", the
main reason being that he was physically abused

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:07.250
by his dad.

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:10.300
This additional backstory for Henry would
have been in keeping with the character's

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.370
abusive father in Stephen King's novel.

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:16.380
But, although Muschietti wanted to include
Henry's backstory in the film, he found that

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:20.720
in test screenings, people didn't react as
he expected and didn't seem interested in

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:25.210
the background to the bully's sadistic behaviour,
so he decided to cut the scenes from the final

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:26.210
film.

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:30.430
Still, while the scenes were deleted that
made clear Henry's abuse by his father behind

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:34.500
closed doors, one scene which hints at his
father's behaviour did make it into the final

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:38.070
movie, and that's the scene where Henry shoots
his dad's gun.

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:42.450
Two other scenes that the director wanted
to include in the shoot ended up being cut

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:47.810
from filming as they needed more CGI and the
movie was on a tight budget of around 35 million

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:48.810
dollars.

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:52.780
The first scene given the chop was a flashback
that showed the first time when the evil entity

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:55.510
It came into contact with human beings.

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:59.050
The scene Muschietti could be referencing
appears in some of the original scripts for

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:04.420
the movie and shows a young mother in 17th-century
Derry making a deal with Pennywise, who tells

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:07.710
her that unless she gives him her baby, he'll
kill everyone.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:11.769
She strikes the deal and so the cycle of IT
in Derry, Maine begins.

00:02:11.769 --> 00:02:15.830
In the new movie, we do see Ben and the Losers'
Club looking at photos and articles about

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:20.120
Old Derry, which indicate that IT has been
around a long time, but we still don't know

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:25.290
exactly where the malevolent creature actually
came from or any real details about IT's background.

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:29.239
Muschietti has said that in the sequel, he
wants the character of Mike to play a more

00:02:29.239 --> 00:02:33.019
"active" part in discovering the answer to
defeating Pennywise.

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:36.879
An idea he's looking at is for Mike to become
addicted to a substance which then opens up

00:02:36.879 --> 00:02:40.849
his mind and allows him to see Pennywise in
the Macroverse.

00:02:40.849 --> 00:02:44.809
This would be a spin on the chapter in King's
novel where the Losers' Club build a Native-American

00:02:44.809 --> 00:02:48.760
smokehole in the hopes that they'll have a
vision that will uncover the truth about IT,

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:52.620
which is what happens to Mike and Richie who
end up having hallucinations that reveal the

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:54.620
origins of IT.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:58.849
The second scene that was cut from filming
was a dream sequence that took place in Derry.

00:02:58.849 --> 00:03:03.099
Basically, Bill is leaning on a bridge, spitting
into the stream below, when out of nowhere

00:03:03.099 --> 00:03:05.269
he spots a balloon reflected in the water.

00:03:05.269 --> 00:03:09.519
At that point, he looks up and sees that there's
a whole load of balloons above him, and then

00:03:09.519 --> 00:03:14.849
he notices that there are also body parts;
and as the shot pulls out wider, he sees there

00:03:14.849 --> 00:03:18.209
are actually dozens of dead kids just floating
in the air.

00:03:18.209 --> 00:03:21.889
Given that Muschietti has talked about this
floating bodies scene and Pennywise's origin

00:03:21.889 --> 00:03:25.569
scene as being 'postponed', we might well
see both of them in the sequel.

00:03:25.569 --> 00:03:29.939
To be honest, though, I'm not sorry that Bill's
dream-like scene of floating bodies ended

00:03:29.939 --> 00:03:34.230
up being cancelled in the first movie as I
think that seeing that would have taken away

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:38.829
from the impact of the last part of the film
where we finally see Pennywise's subterranean

00:03:38.829 --> 00:03:42.989
lair and the many dead bodies floating around
the hoard of toys and belongings from his

00:03:42.989 --> 00:03:45.450
various victims.

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:49.780
Another casualty of keeping the movie on budget
was a scene featuring the Black Spot, which

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:53.180
in King's novel was a club mainly frequented
by black customers.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:57.590
An early script included a flashback to the
club which was burned down by racists during

00:03:57.590 --> 00:04:00.060
one of Pennywise's recurring reigns of terror.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:03.500
But in the final movie, the Black Spot is
merely mentioned in passing.

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:07.749
Still, although it was cut from the first
movie, according to producer Barbara Muschietti,

00:04:07.749 --> 00:04:11.629
their current plan is to open the sequel with
a sequence dealing with events at the Black

00:04:11.629 --> 00:04:13.040
Spot.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:16.970
We'll have to wait a while to see if that's
what happens, but in the meantime, what I

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:21.080
do think is interesting in this movie is the
way the filmmakers have chosen to change Mike's

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:22.790
backstory from the novel.

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:26.900
In the film, Mike's parents died in a house
fire, and the encounter he experiences with

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:31.630
Pennywise begins with a fire and hands desperately
reaching around a chained door, trying to

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:32.630
get free.

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:36.910
So, I'm thinking that in the sequel, the filmmakers
may connect in some way the death of Mike's

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:41.470
parents with the tragedy at the Black Spot,
which in the novel was witnessed by Mike's

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:42.910
father.

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:46.720
Mike first encounters Pennywise when he goes
to make a delivery to a butcher's shop in

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:51.970
Derry and, after the delivery door bursts
open, he sees the clown's evil yellow eyes

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:55.470
from behind a plastic curtain in the meat
storage area.

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:59.100
And there's some behind-the-scenes footage
of an alternate or extended scene where Pennywise

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:03.660
is shown thrashing around and feasting on
what looks like a body behind the plastic

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:04.660
screen.

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:10.670
Check out the deleted moment here.

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:17.200
Now, this scene of Pennywise devouring a body
at the butcher's would perhaps have added

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:21.770
more shock value to Mike's first encounter
with IT, though you could argue that just

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:25.500
seeing him standing there in the final film
is actually more creepy.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:30.711
But I think the reason this moment may have
been cut is that if you notice to the right

00:05:30.711 --> 00:05:33.770
of Pennywise, there's a yellow figure which
gives the impression it might be Georgie hanging

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:35.390
in his yellow raincoat.

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:40.200
In the final film, Georgie has gone missing
but no body has been found, so Bill continues

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:43.930
to believe his little brother could still
be alive, which is a major motivation for

00:05:43.930 --> 00:05:45.080
him throughout the movie.

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:50.120
So, by deleting this extra moment of Pennywise
at the butcher's, it helps the film preserve

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:53.360
that air of mystery around Georgie's disappearance.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:57.650
I've already talked about The Black Spot scene
which was in an early script but ended up

00:05:57.650 --> 00:05:58.650
being cut.

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:03.020
Well, that screenplay was actually written
by Cary Fukunaga and Chase Palmer.

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:08.360
In fact, Fukunaga was also the film's original
director before he quit the project in 2015

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:12.490
over creative differences with the studio,
which led to the Muschietti siblings taking

00:06:12.490 --> 00:06:15.400
over as director and producer of the final
movie.

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:19.390
Palmer and Fukunaga still retain the first
two screenplay credits, and the final movie

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:23.670
is still significantly based on their script,
however, the story does appear to have changed

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:27.110
somewhat in tone and some of the more graphic
scenes from their script have either been

00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:30.180
toned down or cut from the final film.

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:34.240
One example of the more shocking tone that
Fukunaga was going for was Stan's first scene

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:35.310
at the synagogue.

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:39.730
In the final film, Stan has an unsettling
encounter with IT in the form of strange woman

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:40.910
from a painting.

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:45.140
However, in Fukunaga's script, Stan excuses
himself from the Rabbi as he needs to go to

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:49.480
the toilet, and when he can't find the bathroom,
he decides to go inside the Mikveh, a place

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:53.180
for ritual cleansing used, for example, by
women after their period.

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:57.150
As he's about to pee in the pool, a naked
woman rises out of the water and starts to

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:59.000
tempt him while she touches herself.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:03.889
However, she's actually a rotting, bleeding
corpse covered in sores and, as she emerges

00:07:03.889 --> 00:07:07.330
from the water, she says to Stan, "Come float
with me".

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:11.270
Also, in the final movie, there's a scene
where Bev's father appears to be about to

00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:15.540
sexually assault his daughter, something it's
implied he's done in the past.

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:18.760
But in Fukunaga's screenplay, there's little
room left for doubt.

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:22.889
For example, in his script, Bev's father throws
her into his bedroom; demands to know whether

00:07:22.889 --> 00:07:28.420
she's still a virgin and orders her to undress;
and then begins to talk in a Pennywise voice,

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:29.830
"You'll like it, Bevvie.

00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:31.100
Be like you're floating."

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:36.150
There are also reports of other graphic content
in Fukunaga's script involving young characters

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:40.220
such as the bully Henry committing bestiality
with a sheep, however, this doesn't appear

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:43.950
in the scripts that emerged after the first
trailer was released, so it's unclear whether

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:45.520
the reports are accurate.

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:50.220
After he quit the project, Fukunaga said in
an interview with Variety that New Line Cinema

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:54.340
were watering down the movie to make it more
conventional and inoffensive, which he said

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:57.200
was impossible to do with a Stephen King novel.

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:00.410
As for the Muschietti siblings, they've said
that they weren't prevented from pursuing

00:08:00.410 --> 00:08:04.930
adult themes in the movie, however, they added
that they had personal limits for how far

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:07.220
they wanted to push some scenes or ideas.

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:11.491
For instance, in an interview with Mad Movies
magazine, they said they cut a scene of a

00:08:11.491 --> 00:08:16.160
kid being thrown into the toilet after having
his back broken, because they felt it was

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:19.330
"really too much" to portray visually.

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:23.230
Speaking of things that are way "too much",
that seems to be the general consensus of

00:08:23.230 --> 00:08:28.139
the filmmakers on that controversial WTF scene
from King's original novel which features

00:08:28.139 --> 00:08:31.780
a young Bev having sex with each of the other
young Losers in turn.

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:36.379
Both the Muschiettis and Fukunaga decided
to steer clear of portraying this moment which

00:08:36.379 --> 00:08:40.439
happens in the book after the Losers have
defeated IT but are lost trying to find their

00:08:40.439 --> 00:08:42.169
way out of the sewers.

00:08:42.169 --> 00:08:45.839
King has defended the scene saying that he
"wasn't really thinking of the sexual aspect

00:08:45.839 --> 00:08:50.209
of it" and that "the book dealt with childhood
and adulthood" and that the "sexual act connected"

00:08:50.209 --> 00:08:51.209
the two.

00:08:51.209 --> 00:08:54.410
The Muschetti siblings have explained they
didn't think the scene was necessary for the

00:08:54.410 --> 00:08:58.790
story and it wouldn't have worked on screen,
and they also added that the blood oath the

00:08:58.790 --> 00:09:02.610
Losers make at the end is a much more meaningful
way to show how the group have united and

00:09:02.610 --> 00:09:05.459
matured enough to conquer their fears and
IT.

00:09:05.459 --> 00:09:10.579
In his script, Fukunaga actually did a totally
non-sexual version of the scene which showed

00:09:10.579 --> 00:09:15.009
Bev stopping the Losers from bickering and
forcing them to focus by simply holding each

00:09:15.009 --> 00:09:17.399
of their faces in her hands, one-by-one.

00:09:17.399 --> 00:09:23.220
There isn't an end-credits scene in IT, but
there is a little maniacal laugh by Pennywise

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:27.050
at the very end of the credits, teasing that
IT is still out there and will be back in

00:09:27.050 --> 00:09:28.050
Chapter 2.

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:32.470
However, there were rumours of a short scene
involving Jessica Chastain as the older Bev,

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:36.350
who 27 years later, receives a message that
IT has returned.

00:09:36.350 --> 00:09:39.990
Although producer Barbara Muschietti says
this didn't happen, she admits they'd love

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:44.579
Jessica Chastain to play the older Bev in
the sequel, especially given their experience

00:09:44.579 --> 00:09:48.699
working with her on their debut feature, Mama,
and the fact that Chastain looks similar to

00:09:48.699 --> 00:09:51.370
Sophia Lillis who plays young Bev.

00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:55.720
Another actress many would like to see play
the adult Bev is Amy Adams, and I thought

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:59.519
there was a really interesting nod to that
possibility in the movie when Bev puts on

00:09:59.519 --> 00:10:04.180
the pharmacist's glasses and he tells her
she looks a lot like Lois Lane, who of course

00:10:04.180 --> 00:10:07.399
has been played on the big screen most recently
by Amy Adams.

00:10:07.399 --> 00:10:12.279
So, what did you think of IT and is there
anything cut or deleted that you wish had

00:10:12.279 --> 00:10:13.430
been in the movie?

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:16.200
And what do you want to see happen in the
sequel?

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:20.529
Let me know in the comments below and be sure
to subscribe to my channel as well for a chance

00:10:20.529 --> 00:10:22.300
to win a Pennywise Funko Pop.

